{
  "tema": 8579,
  "slug": "tomar-comida-bebida",
  "titulo": "Tomar for having a drink (and comer for food)",
  "subtitulo": "«Tomo un café y como unos tacos.»",
  "foto": "t06.jpg",
  "regla": "For _having_ a drink, use tomar (or beber), never tener. For _having_ food, use comer or the meal verbs desayunar, comer, cenar. Tener means to own or to possess.",
  "comparada_kwiziq": null,
  "secciones": [
    {
      "id": "regla",
      "titulo": "The rule",
      "bloques": [
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "English uses _have_ for food and drink: _have a coffee_, _have lunch_. Spanish **tener** only means _to own_, so it doesn't work here."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "tabla",
          "encabezados": [
            "English",
            "Spanish"
          ],
          "filas": [
            [
              "_have a drink, a coffee, a beer_",
              "**tomar** (or **beber**) un café"
            ],
            [
              "_have something to eat_",
              "**comer** algo"
            ],
            [
              "_have breakfast_",
              "**desayunar**"
            ],
            [
              "_have lunch_ (main midday meal)",
              "**comer**"
            ],
            [
              "_have dinner_",
              "**cenar**"
            ],
            [
              "_have a drink / a snack, go out for one_",
              "**tomar algo**"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "**Tomar** is the everyday verb for drinks in Mexico; **beber** sounds a bit more formal or written."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "**Tomar** on its own often means _to drink alcohol_: **¿Tomas?** = _Do you drink?_"
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "**Tomar** also covers medicine: **tomar una pastilla**."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "In Mexico **la comida** is the main meal of the day (around 2–4 pm), so **comer** is also _to have lunch_."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ejemplos",
      "titulo": "Examples",
      "bloques": [
        {
          "tipo": "ejemplos",
          "lista": [
            {
              "es": "¿Quieres ==tomar== un café?",
              "en": "Do you want to have a coffee?"
            },
            {
              "es": "Después del entrenamiento ==tomo== mucha agua.",
              "en": "After training I drink a lot of water."
            },
            {
              "es": "Vamos a ==tomar algo== el viernes.",
              "en": "Let's go for a drink on Friday."
            },
            {
              "es": "Hoy ==desayuné== chilaquiles.",
              "en": "Today I had chilaquiles for breakfast."
            },
            {
              "es": "¿A qué hora ==comen== ustedes?",
              "en": "What time do you have lunch?"
            },
            {
              "es": "Anoche ==cenamos== tacos en la calle Sexta.",
              "en": "Last night we had tacos for dinner on Sexta street."
            },
            {
              "es": "No ==tomo==, gracias; voy a manejar.",
              "en": "I don't drink, thanks; I'm driving."
            },
            {
              "es": "==Toma== una pastilla para el dolor.",
              "en": "Take a pill for the pain."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
